== The Excellent Nature of Divine Things

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[II. The first objective ground of gracious affections is the transcendently excellent and amiable nature of divine things as they are in themselves, and not any conceived relation they bear to self or self-interest.]

I say that the supremely excellent nature of divine things is the #emph[first,] or #emph[primary and original,] objective foundation of the spiritual affections of true saints. For I do not suppose that every relation divine things bear to the saints themselves and to their own particular interest is wholly excluded from all influence in their gracious affections. This may have, and indeed does have, a secondary and consequential influence in those affections that are truly holy and spiritual, as I shall show shortly.

It was observed before that the affection of love is, as it were, the fountain of all affection, and in particular that Christian love is the fountain of all gracious affections. Now the divine excellence and glory of God and Jesus Christ, of the word of God, the works of God, the ways of God, and so on, is the primary reason why a true saint loves these things. It is not any supposed share he has in them, or any conceived benefit he has received or will receive from them, or any such imagined relation they bear to his interest, so that self-love could properly be said to be the first foundation of his love to these things.

Some say that all love arises from self-love, and that it is impossible in the nature of things for any man to have any love to God, or to any other being, unless love to himself is the foundation of it. But I humbly suppose that they say so for lack of consideration. They argue that whoever loves God, and so desires his glory or the enjoyment of him, desires these things as his own happiness. The glory of God, and the beholding and enjoying of his perfections, are considered as things #emph[agreeable to him,] tending to make him happy. He places his happiness in them and desires them as things which, if they were obtained, would be delightful to him, or would fill him with delight and joy, and so make him happy. And so, they say, it is from self-love, or a desire for his own happiness, that he desires God to be glorified and desires to behold and enjoy his glorious perfections. But then they ought to consider a little further and ask how the man came #emph[to place his happiness] in God’s being glorified, and in contemplating and enjoying God’s perfections. There is no doubt that once God’s glory and the beholding of his perfections have become so agreeable to him that he places his highest happiness in these things, he will desire them as he desires his own happiness. But how did these things come to be so agreeable to him that he counts it his highest happiness to glorify God, and so on? Is not this the fruit of love? A man must first love God, or have his heart united to him, before he will count God’s good his own, and before he will desire the glorifying and enjoying of God as his happiness. It is not strong arguing to say that because, #emph[after] a man has his heart united to God in love, he desires God’s glory and the enjoyment of him as his own happiness #emph[as a fruit of this,] therefore a desire for this happiness of his own must be the #emph[cause and foundation] of his love. It would be just as strong to argue that because a father begot a son, therefore his son certainly begot him. Suppose that #emph[after] a man loves God, and has his heart so united to him as to look on God as his chief good and on God’s good as his own, it is a #emph[consequence and fruit of this] that even self-love, or love for his own happiness, causes him to desire the glorifying and enjoying of God. It does not follow from this that this very exercise of self-love went #emph[before] his love to God, and that his love to God was a #emph[consequence and fruit of that.] Something else, entirely distinct from self-love, might be the cause of this: namely, a change made in the views of his mind and the relish of his heart, by which he perceives a beauty, glory and supreme good in God’s nature as it is in itself. This may be the thing that first draws his heart to God and causes his heart to be united to him, prior to all considerations of his own interest or happiness, although after this, and as a fruit of this, he necessarily seeks his interest and happiness in God.

There is such a thing as a kind of love or affection that a man may have toward persons or things which #emph[does properly arise] from self-love. In it a preconceived relation to himself, or some regard already shown to him by another, or some benefit already received or depended on, #emph[is truly the first foundation] of his love, and what his affection wholly arises from; and this comes before any relish of, or delight in, the nature and qualities inherent in the one beloved, as beautiful and lovable. When the first thing that draws a man’s benevolence to another is the beholding of those qualities and properties in him which appear lovely to him in themselves, and the one who has them worthy of esteem and goodwill on that account, love arises in a very different way than when it first arises from some gift bestowed by another, or depended on from him (as a judge loves and favors a man who has bribed him), or from the relation he supposes another has to him (as a man loves another because he looks on him as his child). When love to another arises in this way, it does truly and properly arise from self-love.

That kind of affection to God or Jesus Christ which properly arises from self-love in this way cannot be a truly gracious and spiritual love, as appears from what has been said already. For self-love is a principle entirely natural, as much in the hearts of devils as of angels; and so surely nothing that is merely the result of it can be supernatural and divine in the way described before #footnote[There is a natural love to Christ, as to one who does you good, and for your own ends; and a spiritual love, for himself, by which the Lord alone is exalted. #emph[Shepard]’s parable of the ten virgins, P. I. P. 25.]. Christ plainly speaks of this kind of love as nothing beyond the love of wicked men (Luke 6:32): “If you#super[+] love those who love you#super[+], what credit is that to you#super[+]? Even sinners love those who love them.” And the devil himself knew that a regard for God so mercenary as to be only for benefits received or depended on (which comes to the same thing) is worthless in the sight of God. Otherwise he would never have used such a slander against #emph[Job] before God, as in Job 1:9–10: “Does Job fear God for nothing? Have You not placed a hedge on every side around him and his household …?” Nor would God ever have implicitly allowed that the objection would have been a good one if the accusation had been true. Yet he did allow it, by allowing the matter to be tried, and #emph[Job] to be so dealt with that it might appear in the outcome whether #emph[Job]’s regard for God was mercenary in this way or not, and by making the proof of the sincerity and goodness of his regard rest on that outcome.

It is unreasonable to think anything other than that the first foundation of a true love to God is that by which he is lovely in himself, or worthy to be loved: the supreme loveliness of his nature. This is certainly what chiefly makes him lovable. What chiefly makes a man, or any creature, lovely is his excellence; and so what chiefly makes God lovely, and must undoubtedly be the chief ground of true love, is his excellence. God’s nature, or the divinity, is infinitely excellent; indeed, it is infinite beauty, brightness and glory itself. But how can that be #emph[true love] of this excellent and lovely nature which is not built on the foundation of #emph[its true loveliness?] How can that be true love of beauty and brightness which is not for the sake of beauty and brightness? How can that be a true prizing of what is in itself infinitely worthy and precious which is not for the sake of its worthiness and preciousness? This infinite excellence of the divine nature, as it is in itself, is the true ground of all that is good in God in any respect. But how can a man truly and rightly love God without loving him for that excellence in him which is the foundation of all that is in any way good or desirable in him? Those whose affection to God is founded first on his profitableness to them begin their affection at the wrong end. They regard God only for the furthest reach of the stream of divine good, where it touches them and reaches their interest. They have no regard for that infinite glory of God’s nature which is the original good and the true fountain of all good, the first fountain of all loveliness of every kind, and so the first foundation of all true love.

A natural principle of self-love may be the foundation of great affections toward God and Christ without any sight of the beauty and glory of the divine nature. There is a certain gratitude that is a merely natural thing. Gratitude is one of the natural affections of the soul of man, as anger is; and there is a gratitude that arises from self-love in very much the same way that anger does. Anger in men is an affection stirred up #emph[against] another, or in opposition to another, for something in him that crosses self-love. Gratitude is an affection one has #emph[toward] another for loving him or gratifying him, or for something in him that suits self-love. And there may be a kind of gratitude without any true or proper love, just as there may be anger without any proper hatred, as in parents toward their children: they may be angry with them and yet at the same time have a strong habitual love for them. This gratitude is the principle at work in wicked men in what Christ declares about them in the sixth chapter of #emph[Luke,] where he says that “sinners love those who love them”; and in what he declares even about the #emph[tax collectors,] who were among the most carnal and profligate sort of men (Matt 5:46). This is the very principle that bribery works on in unjust judges; and it is a principle that even the brute beasts exercise: a dog will love his master who is kind to him. And we see in countless instances that mere nature is enough to stir up gratitude in men, or to affect their hearts with thankfulness to others for kindnesses received, and sometimes toward those against whom they have at the same time a habitual enmity. So #emph[Saul] was more than once greatly affected, and even melted, with gratitude toward #emph[David] for sparing his life, and yet he remained his habitual enemy. And as men may be affected in this way toward men from mere nature, so they may be toward God. Nothing prevents the same self-love from working in the same way toward God as toward men. And we have clear instances of it in Scripture: in the children of #emph[Israel, who sang God’s praises] at the Red Sea #emph[but soon forgot his works;] and in #emph[Naaman] the #emph[Syrian,] who was greatly affected by the miraculous cure of his leprosy, so that his heart was engaged from then on to worship the God who had healed him, and him only, except when it would expose him to ruin in his worldly interest. So too #emph[Nebuchadnezzar] was greatly affected by God’s goodness to him in restoring him to his reason and his kingdom after he had lived with the beasts.

Since gratitude is a natural principle in this way, it makes ingratitude all the more vile and heinous, because it shows a dreadful prevalence of wickedness when wickedness even overbears and suppresses the better principles of human nature. So it is mentioned as evidence of the high degree of wickedness of many of the heathen that they were “without natural affection” (Rom 1:31). But that the lack of gratitude or natural affection is evidence of a high degree of #emph[vice] is no argument that all gratitude and natural affection has the nature of #emph[virtue] or #emph[saving grace.]

Self-love, through the exercise of a merely natural gratitude, may be the foundation of a sort of love to God in many ways. A kind of love may arise from a false notion of God that men have been brought up in, or have somehow absorbed: as though he were only goodness and mercy, and had no avenging justice; or as though the exercises of his goodness were necessary, and not free and sovereign; or as though his goodness depended on what is in them and were, as it were, constrained by them. On such grounds as these men may love a God of their own forming in their imaginations, when they are far from loving such a God as reigns in heaven.

Again, self-love may be the foundation of an affection in men toward God through a great insensibility of their state with regard to God, and for lack of conviction of conscience to make them feel how dreadfully they have provoked God to anger. They have no sense of the heinousness of sin as against God, nor of the infinite and terrible opposition of God’s holy nature to it. And so, having formed in their minds such a God as suits them, and thinking God to be such a one as themselves, who favors them and agrees with them, they may like him very well and feel a sort of love to him, when they are far from loving the true God. And men’s affections may be much moved toward God from self-love by some remarkable outward benefits received from God, as it was with #emph[Naaman, Nebuchadnezzar,] and the children of #emph[Israel] at the #emph[Red Sea.]

Again, a very high affection toward God may arise in men, and often does, from an opinion of God’s favor and love to them as the first foundation of their love to him. After awakenings and distress through fears of hell, they may suddenly get a notion, through some impression on their imagination, or an immediate suggestion, with or without texts of Scripture, or by some other means, that God loves them, has forgiven their sins and made them his children. This is the first thing that causes their affections to flow toward God and Jesus Christ. And then, after this and on this foundation, many things in God may appear lovely to them, and Christ may seem excellent. If such persons are asked whether God appears lovely and lovable in himself, they would perhaps readily answer yes. Yet in truth, if the matter is strictly examined, this good opinion of God was purchased and paid for before they ever gave it, in the distinguishing and infinite benefits they imagined they had received from God. They allow God to be lovely in himself in no other way than this: that he has forgiven them and accepted them, loves them above most in the world, has pledged to use all his infinite power and wisdom in advancing, dignifying and exalting them, and will do for them just what they would have him do. Once they are firm in this persuasion, it is easy to acknowledge God and Christ to be lovely and glorious, and to wonder at them and extol them. It is easy for them to acknowledge Christ to be a lovely person, the best in the world, when they are first firm in the belief that he, though Lord of the universe, is captivated with love to them, has his heart swallowed up in them, prizes them far above most of their neighbors, loved them from eternity, died for them, and will make them reign with him in eternal glory in heaven. When this is the case with carnal men, their very lusts will make him seem lovely. Pride itself will prejudice them in favor of what they call Christ. Selfish, proud man naturally calls that lovely which greatly contributes to his interest and gratifies his ambition.

And as persons of this sort begin, so they go on. Their affections are raised from time to time, primarily on this foundation of self-love and an imagined notion of God’s love to them. Many have a false notion of communion with God, as though it were carried on by impulses and whispers and outward representations made immediately to their imagination. These things they often have, and they take them to be manifestations of God’s great love to them and evidences of their high exaltation above others of mankind; and so their affections are often set going again.

But the exercises of true and holy love in the saints arise in another way. They do not first see that God loves them and then see that he is lovely. They first see that God is lovely, and that Christ is excellent and glorious, and their hearts are first captivated with this view. The exercises of their love usually begin here from time to time, and arise primarily from these views; and then, as a consequence, they see God’s love and great favor to them #footnote[There is a seeing of Christ after a man believes, which is Christ in his love, and so on. But I speak of that first sight of him that precedes the second act of faith; and it is an intuitive, or real, sight of him as he is in his glory. #emph[Shepard]’s parable of the ten virgins, Part I. P. 74.]. The saint’s affections begin with God, and self-love has a hand in these affections only consequentially and secondarily. False affections, on the contrary, begin with self, and an acknowledgment of an excellence in God, and being affected by it, is only consequential and dependent. In the love of the true saint God is the lowest foundation: the love of the excellence of his nature is the foundation of all the affections that come afterward, in which self-love has its part as a handmaid. The hypocrite, on the contrary, lays himself at the bottom of all as the first foundation, and lays God on top as the superstructure; and even his acknowledgment of God’s glory itself depends on his regard for his private interest.

Self-love may influence men so that they are affected not only by God’s kindness to them individually, but also by his kindness to them as parts of a community. A natural principle of self-love, without any other principle, may be enough to make a man concerned for the interest of the nation he belongs to. For instance, in the present war, self-love may make natural men rejoice at our nation’s successes and be sorry for its setbacks, since they have a stake in it as members of the body. In the same way these natural principles may extend further, even to the world of mankind, and men might be affected by the benefits the inhabitants of the earth have beyond those of the inhabitants of other planets, if we knew that there were such and knew how it was with them. So this principle may cause men to be affected by the benefits mankind has received beyond the fallen angels. And so, from this principle, men may be much affected by the wonderful goodness of God to mankind, by his great goodness in giving his Son to die for fallen man, by the marvelous love of Christ in suffering such great things for us, and by the great glory they hear God has provided in heaven for us. They look on themselves as persons who have a stake and a share in it, being members of this species of creatures so highly favored. The same principle of natural gratitude may influence men here as in the case of personal benefits.

But what I have said by no means implies that all gratitude to God is a merely natural thing, and that there is no such thing as a spiritual gratitude, which is a holy and divine affection. It implies no more than that there is a gratitude which is merely natural, and that when persons have affections toward God only or primarily for benefits received, their affection is only the exercise of a natural gratitude. There is no doubt such a thing as a gracious gratitude, which differs greatly from all the gratitude natural men experience. It differs in the following respects:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. True gratitude or thankfulness to God for his kindness to us arises from a foundation laid before, of love to God for what he is in himself; a natural gratitude has no such prior foundation. The gracious stirrings of grateful affection to God for kindness received always come from a stock of love already in the heart, established in the first place on other grounds, namely God’s own excellence; and so the affections are disposed to flow out on occasions of God’s kindness. The saint has seen the glory of God, and his heart has been overcome by it and captivated into a supreme love to him on that account; by this his heart becomes tender and easily affected by kindnesses received. If a man has no love for another, gratitude may still be moved by some extraordinary kindness, as in #emph[Saul] toward #emph[David.] But this is not the same kind of thing as a man’s gratitude to a dear friend whom his heart already held in high esteem and love, so that by this means his heart became tender toward him, more easily affected with gratitude, and affected in another way. Self-love is not excluded from a gracious gratitude: the saints love God for his kindness to them, as in Ps 116:1: “I love the #smallcaps[Lord], for He has heard my voice—my appeal for mercy.” But something else is included as well: another love prepares the way and lays the foundation for these grateful affections.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. In a gracious gratitude men are affected by the attribute of God’s goodness and free grace, not only as they are concerned in it, or as it affects their interest, but as a part of the glory and beauty of God’s nature. That wonderful and unparalleled grace of God which is manifested in the work of redemption, and shines out in the face of Jesus Christ, is infinitely glorious in itself, and appears so to the angels. It is a great part of the moral perfection and beauty of God’s nature. It would be glorious whether it were exercised toward us or not; and the saint who exercises a gracious thankfulness for it sees it to be so and delights in it as such. Yet his own stake in it serves all the more to engage his mind and raise his attention and affection. Self-love here assists as a handmaid, serving higher principles: it leads the mind out to view and contemplate it, engages and fixes the attention, and heightens the joy and love. God’s kindness to them is a mirror God sets before them in which to behold the beauty of the attribute of his goodness. By this means the exercises and displays of this attribute are brought near to them and set right before them. So in a holy thankfulness to God, the stake our interest has in God’s goodness is not the first foundation of our being affected by it. That foundation was laid in the heart before, in that stock of love to God for his excellence in himself which makes the heart tender and open to such impressions from his goodness to us. Nor is our own interest, or the benefits we have received, the only or the chief objective ground of the present exercises of the affection. That ground is God’s goodness, as part of the beauty of his nature; although the manifestations of that lovely attribute, set immediately before our eyes in its exercise for us, are the special occasion of the mind’s attention to that beauty at that time, and serve to fix the attention and heighten the affection.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Some may perhaps be ready to object to all that has been said from the text 1 John 4:19, “We love Him because He first loved us,” as though this implied that God’s love to the true saints were the #emph[first foundation] of their love to him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In answer to this I would observe that the apostle’s purpose in these words is to magnify the love of God to us on this ground: that he loved us while we had no love for him. Anyone who compares this verse and the two following with verses 9, 10 and 11 will see this. And the apostle proves that God loved us when we had no love for him by this argument: that God’s love to the elect is the ground of their love to him. And it is so in three ways. 1. The saints’ love to God is the fruit of God’s love to them, as it is the gift of that love. God gave them a spirit of love to him because he loved them from eternity. In this respect God’s love to his elect is the first foundation of their love to him, as it is the foundation of their regeneration and of the whole of their redemption. 2. The exercises and displays God has made of his wonderful love to sinful men by Jesus Christ in the work of redemption are one of the chief manifestations God has made of the glory of his moral perfection, to both angels and men; and so this is one main objective ground of the love of both to God, which agrees well with what was said before. 3. God’s love to a particular elect person, shown by his conversion, is a great manifestation to him of God’s moral perfection and glory, and a proper occasion for stirring up the love of holy gratitude, in agreement with what was said before. And that the saints love God in these respects because he first loved them fully answers the purpose of the apostle’s argument in that place. So no good argument can be drawn from this text against a spiritual and gracious love in the saints that arises primarily from the excellence of divine things as they are in themselves, and not from any conceived relation they bear to the saints’ interest.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as it is with the love of the saints, so it is with their joy and spiritual delight and pleasure. Its first foundation is not any consideration or conception of their share in divine things. It consists primarily in the sweet delight their minds have in viewing or contemplating the divine and holy beauty of these things as they are in themselves. And this is indeed the very main difference between the joy of the hypocrite and the joy of the true saint. The former rejoices in himself; self is the first foundation of his joy. The latter rejoices in God. The hypocrite has his mind pleased and delighted, in the first place, with his own privilege and the happiness he supposes he has attained or will attain. True saints have their minds, in the first place, inexpressibly pleased and delighted with the sweet ideas of the glorious and lovely nature of the things of God. This is the spring of all their delights and the cream of all their pleasures; it is the joy of their joy. This sweet and ravishing delight they have in viewing the beautiful and delightful nature of divine things is the foundation of the joy they have afterward in considering that these things are theirs. But the affections of hypocrites depend on one another in the opposite order. They first rejoice, and are elated, that God makes so much of them; and then, on that ground, he seems in a way lovely to them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The first foundation of the delight a true saint has in God is God’s own perfection; and the first foundation of the delight he has in Christ is Christ’s own beauty. Christ appears in himself the chief among ten thousand, and altogether lovely. The way of salvation by Christ is a delightful way to him because of the sweet and wonderful manifestations of the divine perfections in it. The holy doctrines of the gospel, by which God is exalted and man abased, holiness honored and promoted, sin greatly disgraced and discouraged, and free and sovereign love manifested, are glorious doctrines in his eyes and sweet to his taste, prior to any conception of his share in these things. Indeed the saints rejoice in their share in God, and that Christ is theirs, and they have great reason to. But this is not the first spring of their joy. They first rejoice in God as glorious and excellent in himself, and then secondarily rejoice that so glorious a God is theirs. They first have their hearts filled with sweetness from the view of Christ’s excellence, and the excellence of his grace, and the beauty of the way of salvation by him; and then they have a secondary joy that so excellent a Savior and such excellent grace are theirs. #footnote[Dr. #emph[Owen,] on the SPIRIT, p. 199, speaking of a common work of the Spirit, says: The effects of this work on the mind, which is the first subject affected by it, do not go so far as to give it delight, pleasure and satisfaction in the lovely spiritual nature and excellences of the things revealed to it. The true nature of saving illumination consists in this: it gives the mind such a direct, intuitive insight into spiritual things, and view of them, that in their own spiritual nature they suit, please and satisfy it; so that it is transformed into them, cast into their mold, and rests in them (Rom 6:17; 12:2; 1 Cor 2:13–14; 2 Cor 3:18; 4:6). The work we have insisted on does not reach this far. For whatever manifestation of spiritual things is made to the mind in it, the mind does not find an immediate, direct, spiritual excellence in them, but only with respect to some benefit or advantage to be gained by means of them. It will not give such a spiritual insight into the mystery of God’s grace by Jesus Christ, called his glory shining in the face of Christ (2 Cor 4:6), that the soul, in its first direct view of it, should wonder at it, delight in it, approve it, and find spiritual solace and refreshment in it for what it is in itself. But it imparts such a light, such a knowledge, that a man may like it well in its effects, as a way of mercy and salvation.] But what is the true saint’s superstructure is the hypocrite’s foundation. When hypocrites hear of the wonderful things of the gospel, of God’s great love in sending his Son, of Christ’s dying love to sinners, and the great things Christ has purchased and promised to the saints, and hear these things set out in a lively and eloquent way, they may hear with a great deal of pleasure and be lifted up by what they hear. But if their joy is examined, it will be found to have no other foundation than this: they look on these things as theirs. All this exalts them. They love to hear of the great love of Christ so vastly distinguishing some from others; for self-love, and even pride itself, makes them crave great distinction from others. No wonder that, in this confident opinion of their own good state, they feel well under such doctrine and are pleased in the highest degree to hear how much God and Christ make of them. So their joy is really a joy in themselves, and not in God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And because the joy of hypocrites is in themselves, it comes about that in their rejoicings and elations they usually keep their eye on themselves. Having received what they call spiritual manifestations or experiences, their minds are taken up with them, wondering at their own experiences. What they are chiefly taken with and elated by is not the glory of God or the beauty of Christ, but the beauty of their experiences. They keep thinking to themselves, What a good experience this is! What a great manifestation this is! What wonderful things I have met with! And so they put their experiences in the place of Christ and his beauty and fullness. Instead of rejoicing in Christ Jesus, they rejoice in their wonderful experiences. Instead of feeding and feasting their souls on the view of what is outside them, namely the innate, sweet, refreshing loveliness of the things set forth in the gospel, their eyes are off these things, or at least they view them only, as it were, sideways. The object that fixes their contemplation is their experience; and they are feeding their souls, and feasting a selfish principle, on a view of their manifestations. They take more comfort in their manifestations than in Christ manifested. This is the true notion of living on experiences and frames; it is not the using of experiences as the signs on which they rely for evidence of their good state, which some call living on experiences. Yet it is very noticeable that some of those who call it so are the most notorious for living on experiences in the true sense of the term.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The affections of hypocrites very often run this way. They are first much affected by some impression on their imagination, or some impulse, which they take to be an immediate suggestion or testimony from God of his love and their happiness and high privilege in some respect, with or without a text of Scripture. They are mightily taken with this as a great manifestation, and from it high affections arise. And when their affections are raised, they view those high affections and call them great and wonderful experiences; and they have a notion that God is greatly pleased with those affections. This affects them still more, and so they are affected by their affections. And so their affections rise higher and higher, until at times they are completely swallowed up; and self-conceit and a fierce zeal rise along with them. And all is built like a castle in the air, on no other foundation than imagination, self-love and pride.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as the thoughts of persons of this sort are, so is their talk; for out of the abundance of their heart their mouth speaks. As in their high affections they keep their eye on the beauty of their experiences and the greatness of their attainments, so they are great talkers about themselves. The true saint, when under great spiritual affections, is ready from the fullness of his heart to speak much of God and his glorious perfections and works, of the beauty and loveliness of Christ, and of the glorious things of the gospel. But hypocrites in their high affections talk more of the manifestation than of the thing manifested. They are full of talk about the great things they have met with, the wonderful manifestations they have had, how sure they are of God’s love to them, how safe their condition is, how they know they will go to heaven, and so on.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[A true saint, when he enjoys true manifestations of the sweet glory of God and Christ, has his mind too much captivated and engaged by what he views outside himself to stop at that time to view himself and his own attainments. It would be a distraction and a loss he could not bear to take his eye off the ravishing object of his contemplation in order to survey his own experience, and to spend time thinking to himself what a high attainment this is, and what a good story I now have to tell others. Nor does the pleasure and sweetness of his mind at that time arise chiefly from considering the safety of his state, or anything he sees of his own qualities, experiences or circumstances. It arises from the divine and supreme beauty of what is the object of his direct view outside himself, which sweetly delights and strongly holds his mind.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As the love and joy of hypocrites all come from the source of self-love, so do their other affections: their sorrow for sin, their humiliation and submission, their religious desires and zeal. Everything is, as it were, paid for beforehand, in God’s highly gratifying their self-love and their lusts by making so much of them and exalting them so highly, as things stand in their imagination. It is easy for nature, corrupt as it is, under the notion of being already some of the highest favorites of heaven, and of having a God who so protects them and favors them in their sins, to love this imaginary God who suits them so well, to extol him, submit to him, and be fierce and zealous for him. The high affections of many are all built on the supposition that they are eminent saints. If the opinion they have of themselves were taken away, if they thought they were among the lower rank of saints (though they still supposed themselves to be real saints), their high affections would fall to the ground. If they only saw a little of the sinfulness and vileness of their own hearts, and their deformity in the midst of their best duties and their best affections, it would knock their affections on the head; because their affections are built on self, self-knowledge would destroy them. But truly gracious affections are built elsewhere. They have their foundation outside self, in God and Jesus Christ. And so a discovery of themselves, of their own deformity and the poverty of their experiences, though it will purify their affections, will not destroy them, but in some respects will sweeten and heighten them.]]
